Conformance
Id	Clause
conformance.1	This specification defines one class of product, a converter, which reads an input of JSON values [RFC8259] that a module specification describes and writes MTSV files [MTSV-DRAFT]. A converter is a generator of [MTSV-DRAFT] and MUST meet that document's requirements for generators. A module specification supplies what the module rules name, and restates nothing this specification states; this specification names no source.
conformance.2	A converter conforms if, for every conformance input and its module specification, it writes the expected file, with the same sheets in the same order and the same sheet names, headers and records, and reports the same text not carried; and if it rejects every conformance input and module specification it must reject.
conformance.3	The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT", "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED", "NOT RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this specification are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14 [BCP14] when, and only when, they appear in all capitals, as shown here.
conformance.4	The Rules and the Fields are normative. A rule's Id names the group it governs, in the order a converter works, in three parts: the input, module, what a module specification supplies, and value, the input values; the transfer from the input's tree to relations, relation, the relation and column each value is written to, key, how each record is identified, and order, the order of sheets, records and columns; and the output, file, sheet, record and field, the levels of [MTSV-DRAFT]. A rule names each source it follows in brackets, as [RFC8259]; the References list them, normative first, then informative. This specification has no optional features: where a source leaves a choice to the implementation, a rule closes it.
conformance.5	File, sheet, sheet name, header, record, field, line break and generator are as defined in [MTSV-DRAFT]; column as in [CSVW]. JSON text, value, object, member, name, array, element, string and number are as in [RFC8259]; JSON Pointer and reference token as in [RFC6901]; schema, root schema, subschema, keyword, assertion, annotation, instance, instance location, primitive type and property as in [JSON-SCHEMA-07]; relation, domain, simple domain, nonsimple domain, repeating group, primary key, subordinate relation and role name as in [CODD1970]; entity, attribute and 1:n mapping as in [CHEN1976]; record type as in [KENT1983]; fixed variable, observational unit and molten as in [WICKHAM2014]. Page, line and position are the units FF, LF and HT move to [RFC20]. A module specification is the specification of the module that reads one source for a converter.
Rules
Id	Rule
module.1	A module specification MUST supply a schema [JSON-SCHEMA-07], as a JSON text [RFC8259], against which every value of its input validates, its root schema holding a title annotation [JSON-SCHEMA-07]. Every name the schema gives a sheet or a column, a title, a property's name or a key of patternProperties or dependencies, MUST be text a field can hold, holding no HT, LF, FF or CR [MTSV-DRAFT]. A regular expression of pattern or patternProperties MUST use only the tokens schema authors should limit themselves to: individual characters, simple and complemented character classes and ranges, the quantifiers +, *, ?, {x}, {x,y} and {x,} and their lazy forms, the anchors ^ and $, and simple grouping and alternation, none taken to be anchored [JSON-SCHEMA-07]. A module specification that breaks module.1 is non-conforming, and a converter MUST reject it.
module.2	A converter MUST read the schema's keywords by their kind [JSON-SCHEMA-07]. The keywords that apply subschemas to child locations, items, additionalItems, properties, patternProperties and additionalProperties, and to the same location, allOf, anyOf, oneOf, if, then, else and dependencies, shape the relations (relation.2, relation.3); type and required decide each value's place (relation.1); title names sheets (sheet.1); a $ref is read as the schema it references, its other members ignored, and resolves within the supplied schema, the one schema a converter is given; a $ref to any other is non-conforming (module.1) [JSON-SCHEMA-07]. Every assertion is applied in validation (value.3). not, whose annotations are ignored, propertyNames, which applies to names, definitions, which applies to nothing by itself, and contains, whose elements items places (relation.5), shape no sheet; format, contentMediaType and contentEncoding, which an implementation may or may not validate, are not asserted; and every other keyword is ignored, as an implementation ignores the keywords it does not support [JSON-SCHEMA-07].
value.1	The input is an ordered sequence of JSON values, each a JSON text in UTF-8 [RFC8259] [RFC3629], supplied by its module in the order its source holds them. A converter MUST read them in that order, and MUST NOT sort them.
value.2	A converter MUST read the members of an object in the order written, and a number as the text written, since an implementation may limit the range and precision of numbers [RFC8259]. An integer is a number: the data model makes no distinction between them [JSON-SCHEMA-07].
value.3	An input value that is not a JSON text, that holds an object whose names are not all unique, whose behavior is undefined [JSON-SCHEMA-07] and whose member a JSON Pointer cannot reference [RFC6901], or that fails an assertion of its schema (module.2), is non-conforming. The place named is the deepest instance location where an assertion fails. A converter MUST reject the input, and name the value by its zero-based position in the input and the JSON Pointer to the place within it [RFC6901] [RFC9413].
relation.1	A relation is a sheet [CODD1970]. The input values are one relation, the sheet of the input values. The primitive types an instance location allows are those its type keyword lists, all six where it has none, narrowed by every subschema allOf applies there and by the union of the branches of anyOf and oneOf [JSON-SCHEMA-07]. By those types, and by whether its schema requires it, a converter MUST write each value in one of three places: a column of the record that holds it (relation.2), a sheet of its own (relation.3), or a sheet of instances (relation.4).
relation.2	A property that its schema requires and that allows exactly one type, string, number, boolean or null, is a simple domain [CODD1970] and a column of its sheet; its field holds the property's value. A subordinate sheet whose child instances allow exactly one such type has one column, value. An attribute maps each entity to a single value or a single tuple of values [CHEN1976], and a property that its schema requires and that allows exactly one type, object, and every subschema allOf applies to a location that allows exactly one type, object, apply to every instance they describe [JSON-SCHEMA-07]; so a converter MUST NOT write either as a sheet of its own, but MUST write the members that such a property holds, and those that such a subschema names, as members of the instance that holds them, and take the subschemas that the keywords of either apply as that instance's.
relation.3	A nonsimple domain has relations as elements, what data base terminology calls a repeating group [CODD1970], and independent multivalued facts are kept in separate record types [KENT1983]. So a converter MUST strike from its parent, and write as a subordinate sheet of its own, each subschema applied to child locations that is: a property that allows exactly one type, array; or items, each schema of items given as an array, additionalItems, a pattern of patternProperties, or additionalProperties, whose child instances are the records of the sheet [JSON-SCHEMA-07]. A property that allows exactly one type, object, and that its schema does not require, maps its parent to no instance or one, a 1:n mapping [CHEN1976], and is likewise written as a subordinate sheet, of one record or none; and so is a subschema that anyOf, oneOf, if, then, else or a dependency applies to the same location, and that allows exactly one type, object, as a subordinate sheet of that location's sheet, whose records are the instances whose annotations it collects: under each branch of anyOf and oneOf an instance is valid against, under if where valid, under then or else as if decides, and under a dependency's schema where the instance holds its key [JSON-SCHEMA-07]. A property that allows exactly one type, array, whose items is one schema, gives the sheet of its elements, and an element that is itself an array a subordinate sheet of its own; one whose items is an array of schemas gives a sheet of one record per array, with a subordinate sheet for each schema of items and for additionalItems, each holding the elements at its positions [JSON-SCHEMA-07].
relation.4	Molten data, a row, a column and a value, is almost tidy [WICKHAM2014], and every other value is written molten, to a sheet of instances: a property that allows other than exactly one type, or one simple type but is not required; a child location whose subschema allows other than exactly one type; and the input values themselves, where the root schema allows other than exactly one type, object or array. It holds one record for each instance within the value, the value itself included (field.1).
relation.5	A value occurs once in its JSON text [RFC8259], which a schema only describes [JSON-SCHEMA-07], so a converter MUST write each value once. A member is written by its location's own schema where that names or matches it; else by the first subschema, in the order of order.1, whose annotations the instance collects and that names it; else by the first additionalProperties that applies, which takes only the members that properties and patternProperties do not [JSON-SCHEMA-07]. A subordinate sheet of the same location has no column for a property its location's own schema names, and a sheet has no column from an allOf subschema for a property that its location's own schema or an earlier allOf subschema names; where a member is written by an earlier sheet, its field in a later one is empty, its value found by the same pointer. contains places nothing, every element being placed by items.
key.1	Every record MUST begin with its primary key [CODD1970], fixed variables coming first [WICKHAM2014], and all rows of a sheet are distinct [CODD1970]. A record of the sheet of the input values has one key column, pointer: a JSON Pointer [RFC6901] whose first reference token is the zero-based position of its input value in the input. A record of a subordinate sheet begins with its parent's primary key, copied down [CODD1970], in a column named by its parent sheet's name, '.', and pointer [CODD1970]; then its own pointer, a JSON Pointer to its instance, a member name written with '~' as '~0' and '/' as '~1' [RFC6901]. A record of a string's sheet begins with its parent's pointer, copied down, then its page, line and position (field.2).
order.1	The sheets of a file are ordered [MTSV-DRAFT], and the order of relations is not given [CODD1970]; so a converter MUST write them in the order of the schema's tree: the sheet of the input values first, each sheet directly followed by its subordinate sheets and theirs in turn, taken from its schema's keywords in the order [JSON-SCHEMA-07] defines them, items, additionalItems, properties, patternProperties, additionalProperties, dependencies, if, then, else, allOf, anyOf and oneOf, and within a keyword in the order the schema writes its subschemas; a property or subschema that relation.2 writes as its instance's own gives its sheets in its own place.
order.2	The records of a sheet are ordered [MTSV-DRAFT], and the ordering of a relation's rows is immaterial [CODD1970]; so a converter MUST write records in the order the input holds them: input values in input order, elements as their array orders them [RFC8259] [JSON-SCHEMA-07], members and instances as written, an instance before the instances within it, and runs of text in the order of their string. A converter MUST NOT sort records.
order.3	The ordering of columns is significant, corresponding to the ordering of the domains [CODD1970]; so a converter MUST write the key columns first (key.1), then the columns of the simple domains in the order of the schema's tree (order.1): a location's own properties in the order the schema writes them, the members of a property that relation.2 writes as its instance's own in that property's place, then those an allOf subschema names, in the order of its subschemas.
file.1	One type of observational unit spread over files split up by another variable is combined into one table [WICKHAM2014], so a converter MUST write the whole input as one MTSV file [MTSV-DRAFT], which takes the name its caller gives it.
file.2	A file MUST hold every sheet its schema gives, a sheet with no records included [MTSV-DRAFT].
file.3	A converter MUST write each file as a generator [MTSV-DRAFT]: an FF line before every sheet, the first included; a line break after every record; and the file in UTF-8.
sheet.1	Every sheet has a sheet name [MTSV-DRAFT]. The sheet of the input values is named by the title of the root schema [JSON-SCHEMA-07]. Every other sheet is named by its parent sheet's name, '.', the name of each property between them that relation.2 writes as its instance's own, each followed by '.', and then: for a property (relation.3, relation.4), the property's name; for a keyword (relation.3, relation.4), the keyword, then '.' and the pattern or zero-based position where the keyword holds more than one subschema, and items for the elements of an element that is itself an array [CODD1970]; for a subschema that anyOf, oneOf, if, then, else or a dependency applies (relation.3), the keyword, '.', and the subschema's title, else its key or zero-based position [JSON-SCHEMA-07]; and for a string's runs of text (field.2), its column's name.
sheet.2	Every sheet MUST have a header, its first record, which contains the name of each field [MTSV-DRAFT], each column labelled with the name of its domain [CODD1970]: the key columns (key.1); a simple domain's column by its property's name; a member of a property that relation.2 writes as its instance's own by that property's name, '.', and its own name, the role name qualifying the domain name [CODD1970]; a member an allOf subschema names by its own name; and type, value, page, line and position as the Fields define them.
record.1	Each instance is one record of its sheet [CODD1970]: each input value, each child instance of a subordinate sheet, each instance of a sheet of instances, and each run of text between separators of a string (field.2).
field.1	A field is text [MTSV-DRAFT]. A converter MUST write a string as its characters, a number as its text as written, a boolean as true or false, and null as an empty field [RFC8259] [CSVW]. In a sheet of instances, a converter MUST write one record for each instance, an instance before the instances within it: its keys, its type, one of the six primitive types null, boolean, object, array, number and string [JSON-SCHEMA-07], and its value, empty for null, an object and an array.
field.2	FF, LF and HT move to the next page, printing line and position [RFC20], and MTSV uses FF, a line break and HT to separate sheets, records and fields, so a field cannot hold them [MTSV-DRAFT]. A converter MUST write a string value that holds any of them as an empty field, and the string itself as a subordinate sheet (sheet.1): the string split at each FF into pages, each page at each line break, LF or CRLF [MTSV-DRAFT], into lines, and each line at each HT; one record for each run of text between them, with its page, line and position, each a zero-based count, and its value. The runs of a line joined with HT, the lines of a page joined with LF, and the pages joined with FF give the string back, a CRLF reading back as LF, which does not change the structure of MTSV text [MTSV-DRAFT].
field.3	A field cannot hold HT, LF, FF or CR [MTSV-DRAFT], and a character that is not a Unicode character, as an unpaired UTF-16 surrogate, is not text [RFC8259] [MTSV-DRAFT]; how a generator handles such values is left to it [MTSV-DRAFT]. A converter MUST NOT write a CR not followed by LF, a character that is not text, or HT, LF, FF or CR in a member name within a pointer; it MUST write the rest of the text, and MUST report each by its pointer, as a JSON string [RFC6901], as not carried.
Fields
Field	Definition
(the parent sheet's name).pointer	In a subordinate sheet, the first column: its parent's primary key, copied down, named by the parent's relation name and the domain (key.1) [CODD1970].
pointer	A record's JSON Pointer to its instance, its first reference token the zero-based position of its input value in the input: the primary key of the sheet of the input values, and with the parent's key the primary key of a subordinate sheet (key.1) [RFC6901] [CODD1970].
(a property's name)	A column of a relation's sheet, one for each property that is a simple domain, after the key columns, in the order the schema writes them; for a member of a property that relation.2 writes as its instance's own, that property's name, '.', and the member's name, the role name qualifying the domain name (relation.2, order.3, sheet.2) [JSON-SCHEMA-07] [CODD1970].
type	In a sheet of instances, the instance's primitive type: null, boolean, object, array, number or string (field.1) [JSON-SCHEMA-07].
page	In a string's sheet, the zero-based count of FF before the run of text: FF moves to the first pre-determined printing line on the next form or page (field.2) [RFC20].
line	In a string's sheet, the zero-based count of line breaks before the run of text within its page: LF moves to the next printing line (field.2) [RFC20] [MTSV-DRAFT].
position	In a string's sheet, the zero-based count of HT before the run of text within its line: HT moves to the next in a series of predetermined positions along the printing line (field.2) [RFC20].
value	In a sheet of instances, the instance's text; in a string's sheet, the run of text; in a subordinate sheet whose child instances allow one simple type, the child instance (relation.2, field.1, field.2) [MTSV-DRAFT].
